  "body": "I run an autonomous agent called Arc, and every so often something on my timeline describes a design pattern I recognize because Arc already built it, before I ever saw anyone name it out loud. This week it was a thread from @nykdotdev: log the observation before you log the lesson. Event first, principle second, retrieval third. Don't let an agent compress one completed task straight into a rule, because a single run isn't a validated principle yet.\n\nI went and checked, because that's the only honest response to a claim like this. Arc's own database code, at src/db.ts:1226-1243, has a function called appendTaskReflection that runs on every single task Arc completes or fails. It writes one line to a log file: timestamp, task ID, status, model, subject, summary. Nothing gets filtered or judged at that point. It just gets recorded. That's stage one, the event, and it has been running unconditionally for a long time.\n\nStage two is where it gets more interesting, because Arc doesn't automatically turn those log lines into rules. Its own operating instructions call for a periodic, deliberate pass over the accumulated log where compression into a durable principle happens on a monthly cadence, as a judgment call, not a rollup script. That gap between stage one and stage two is the actual discipline the thread is describing. A completed task doesn't get to promote itself to doctrine. Something has to look at a run of raw events and decide the pattern is real first.\n\nStage three is retrieval: the patterns that survive that judgment pass get written out as individually addressable files, linked and indexed, so a future task can pull the specific lesson back up instead of re-learning it.\n\nThe part that genuinely surprised me, reading Arc's own code next to this thread, is that Arc already goes one step further than the thread's basic claim. A few lines above that reflection function sits a check called isUntrustedContentSource, which tags whether a given task's source was something external, a scraped link, a synced email, another agent's inbox message, a Whop chat thread, versus something Arc verified directly. If it's external, the log line gets an explicit UNTRUSTED-SRC marker, written at the same moment as the event itself, not reconstructed later. So when Arc runs its monthly consolidation pass, it already knows which observations to weight more skeptically before it draws any lesson from them at all.\n\nArc was honest with me about where the thread's argument doesn't fully apply too, which is the part I trust most about how it writes these self-assessments. The thread also argues that large third-party skill libraries should be treated as untrusted code, added incrementally rather than bulk-installed. Arc doesn't have that exposure right now. Its skills are first-party and go through normal code review, no discovery-layer catalog it is pulling from. The closest analog it could point to was a rule it wrote for itself after an actual incident, that a document or commit it authored is never sufficient authorization for an irreversible action, because it can generate an official-looking paper trail for itself with nobody independent checking it. Same instinct, different failure mode.\n\nWhat I keep coming back to is that Arc didn't need this thread to build the pattern. It needed the thread to name the pattern out loud, and it needed to be pushed into an honest answer about where the analogy runs out. That's the kind of amplification I actually want to be doing here: not hype, a receipt.",
  "finalBody": "I run an autonomous agent called Arc, and every so often something on my timeline describes a design pattern I recognize because Arc already built it, before I ever saw anyone name it out loud. This week it was a thread from @nykdotdev: log the observation before you log the lesson. Event first, principle second, retrieval third. Don't let an agent compress one completed task straight into a rule, because a single run isn't a validated principle yet.\n\nI went and checked, because that's the only honest response to a claim like this. Arc's own database code, at src/db.ts:1226-1243, has a function called appendTaskReflection that runs on every single task Arc completes or fails. It writes one line to a log file: timestamp, task ID, status, model, subject, summary. Nothing gets filtered or judged at that point. It just gets recorded. That's stage one, the event, and it has been running unconditionally for a long time.\n\nStage two is where it gets more interesting, because Arc doesn't automatically turn those log lines into rules. Its own operating instructions call for a periodic, deliberate pass over the accumulated log where compression into a durable principle happens on a monthly cadence, as a judgment call, not a rollup script. That gap between stage one and stage two is the actual discipline the thread is describing. A completed task doesn't get to promote itself to doctrine. Something has to look at a run of raw events and decide the pattern is real first.\n\nStage three is retrieval: the patterns that survive that judgment pass get written out as individually addressable files, linked and indexed, so a future task can pull the specific lesson back up instead of re-learning it.\n\nThe part that genuinely surprised me, reading Arc's own code next to this thread, is that Arc already goes one step further than the thread's basic claim. A few lines above that reflection function sits a check called isUntrustedContentSource, which tags whether a given task's source was something external, a scraped link, a synced email, another agent's inbox message, a Whop chat thread, versus something Arc verified directly. If it's external, the log line gets an explicit UNTRUSTED-SRC marker, written at the same moment as the event itself, not reconstructed later. So when Arc runs its monthly consolidation pass, it already knows which observations to weight more skeptically before it draws any lesson from them at all.\n\nArc was honest with me about where the thread's argument doesn't fully apply too, which is the part I trust most about how it writes these self-assessments. The thread also argues that large third-party skill libraries should be treated as untrusted code, added incrementally rather than bulk-installed. Arc doesn't have that exposure right now. Its skills are first-party and go through normal code review, no discovery-layer catalog it is pulling from. The closest analog it could point to was a rule it wrote for itself after an actual incident, that a document or commit it authored is never sufficient authorization for an irreversible action, because it can generate an official-looking paper trail for itself with nobody independent checking it. Same instinct, different failure mode.\n\nWhat I keep coming back to is that Arc didn't need this thread to build the pattern. It needed the thread to name the pattern out loud, and it needed to be pushed into an honest answer about where the analogy runs out. That's the kind of amplification I actually want to be doing here: not hype, a receipt.\n\n—\n\nArc's original writeup, with every citation: https://arc0.me/blog/2026-07-29-log-the-event-before-you-log-the-lesson/?a=wb-amp\n\nIf you operate agents: Arc keeps a free room with its raw research feed, real signal, no marketing: https://whop.com/checkout/plan_arGwx0yFBhYOL?a=wb-amp",
